---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
const plans = [
 { weeks: '4 weeks', name: 'Find your baseline', href: '/4-week-program/', start: 'About 5 seconds or more', fit: 'New to hanging or returning after time away.', job: 'Builds toward a 30-second hang from a 15 to 20 second baseline; repeat weeks 3 and 4 if you start lower.' },
 { weeks: '8 weeks', name: 'Make practice consistent', href: '/8-week-program/', start: 'A comfortable 20 to 30 seconds', fit: 'You can hold a comfortable 20 to 30 seconds.', job: 'Use two four-week blocks to compare sessions and change one variable at a time.' },
 { weeks: '12 weeks', name: 'Review a longer training block', href: '/12-week-program/', start: 'A comfortable 30 seconds or longer', fit: 'You can hold a comfortable 30 seconds or longer and want a longer record alongside your existing training.', job: 'Plan three review points and keep the same assistance category so the record stays useful. Added load or one-arm work is an option.' },
];
const faqs = [
 { q: 'Which dead hang program should I start with?', a: 'Start with the 4-week program if you can hold a bar for about 5 seconds. Move to the 8-week program once you hold a comfortable 20 to 30 seconds, and to the 12-week program at a comfortable 30 seconds or longer. A supported hold is a fine starting variation.' },
 { q: 'How many days a week should I follow a dead hang program?', a: 'Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free. Leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.' },
 { q: 'What is the difference between a dead hang workout and a dead hang program?', a: 'A dead hang workout is one session of sets, holds and rest. A dead hang program arranges sessions over 4, 8 or 12 weeks with review points. The dead hang guide holds the single-session workout plan.' },
 { q: 'Do I need anything besides a pull-up bar?', a: 'No. A secure bar and a safe way to step down are enough. A timer helps, and the free timer and log on this site work offline. Chalk and a mat are optional.' },
 { q: 'Will a dead hang program guarantee a longer hold?', a: 'No. These plans organize practice and give you review points. Hold time depends on the person, so compare your holds with your own earlier holds made under the same conditions.' },
];
---
<Article h1={"Dead hang training programs."} subtitle={"Pick the 4, 8 or 12-week program that matches the hold you can control today. Each one is free, uses 3 sessions a week and does not promise a particular hold time."} crumbs={[{"name":"Training plans"}]} updated={"Updated November 2, 2026"} title="Dead Hang Training Programs: 4-Week, 8-Week & 12-Week Plans" description="Choose a free 4, 8 or 12-week dead hang program. Compare who each suits, sessions per week, sets and rest, then set up a timer and log without signing up." schema={`<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Dead Hang Training Programs: 4-Week, 8-Week & 12-Week Plans","description":"Choose a free 4, 8 or 12-week dead hang program. Compare who each suits, sessions per week, sets and rest, then set up a timer and log without signing up.","dateModified":"2026-11-02","mainEntityOfPage":"https://deadhangs.com/training-programs/","author":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":faqs.map(f=>({"@type":"Question","name":f.q,"acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":f.a}}))})}</script>`}>
<div class="article-body">
<div class="callout"><h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2><ol class="stop-steps"><li><strong>Can hold about 5 seconds:</strong> start with the <a href="/4-week-program/">4-week program</a>.</li><li><strong>Hold a comfortable 20 to 30 seconds:</strong> use the <a href="/8-week-program/">8-week program</a>.</li><li><strong>Hold a comfortable 30 seconds or longer:</strong> use the <a href="/12-week-program/">12-week program</a>.</li><li><strong>Every program:</strong> 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days, 3 sets, 60 to 90 seconds of rest.</li></ol><p>These doses are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p></div>
<h2>Which dead hang program should you choose?</h2><p>Choose by the hold you can control today, not by a claimed population rank. The 4-week program suits beginners, the 8-week program suits a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold and the 12-week program suits a comfortable 30 seconds or longer. A supported hold is a legitimate starting variation. Stop if you have pain and seek suitable advice first.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Dead hang programs compared" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Program</th><th scope="col">Start when you can hold</th><th scope="col">Sessions per week</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>{plans.map(plan=><tr><td><a href={plan.href}>{plan.weeks}</a></td><td>{plan.start}</td><td>3</td><td>{plan.job}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>For a single dead hang session rather than a multi-week program, use the <a href="/deadhang-guide/#workout-plan">dead hang workout plan</a> in the guide.</p>
{plans.map(plan=><section class="plan-option"><p class="dh-eyebrow">{plan.weeks}</p><h3>{plan.name}</h3><p><strong>{plan.fit}</strong> {plan.job}</p><a class="text-link" href={plan.href}>Read the {plan.weeks.replace(' ', '-')} program →</a></section>)}
<h2>What settings should you use in the timer?</h2><p>The training log timer on this site starts with 30 seconds of hanging, 60 seconds of rest and three sets. They are editable timer settings and not a prescription. Shorten the hold, add assistance or use a different rest interval to match your actual ability.</p><p>Save a personal goal if you want it on this device. Record actual holds after training. Avoid treating a timer reaching zero as proof that you completed a set.</p><a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Set up my goal, timer & log</a>
<h2>How does a dead hang program fit your wider training?</h2><p>Dead hangs are one movement. They do not replace a balanced strength and aerobic program. NHS guidance recommends strengthening the major muscle groups on at least two days each week; it does not prescribe a dead-hang routine.</p><p><a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">Read the NHS adult activity guidance</a>, for advice on returning to exercise or exercising with a medical condition.</p>
<section id="print-plan" class="print-planner"><h2>Take your plan to the bar.</h2><p>Write the settings before your session. Fill in actual results afterwards. Keep assisted and unassisted holds separate.</p><div class="planner-lines"><p>Date:</p><p>Bar / grip / assistance:</p><p>Planned hang seconds / rest seconds / sets:</p><p>Actual holds:</p><p>What to repeat or change next time:</p></div><p class="small-copy">Stop if painful. Step down under control. This is a personal record and not a health assessment.</p><button type="button" class="dh-btn print-button">Print blank planner</button></section>
<h2>What equipment does a dead hang program need?</h2><p>Check the bar's instructions, mounting requirements, clearance and load rating. A suitable bar matters more than a longer shopping list.</p><p>The <a href="/equipment-guide/">equipment guide</a> compares mounting options and discloses affiliate links.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>{faqs.map(f=><div class="faq"><h3>{f.q}</h3><p>{f.a}</p></div>)}
</div></Article>
<script>document.querySelector('.print-button')?.addEventListener('click', () => window.print());</script>
